cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or build on this framework, please cite it as below."
title: "The Epoch Project: Epochs as Bounded Invariant-Persistence Intervals"
abstract: >-
  The Epoch Project is a documentation research framework that reframes an
  "epoch" as a bounded interval of reality defined not by duration but by the
  persistence of a verifiable boundary condition - a "soul invariant" (psi):
  checked at every transition in the operational family, conserved by
  construction in the continuous family. It models this as a six-tuple automaton
  E = (S, Sigma, delta, s0, F, psi) observed by a meta-level "Ark," and develops
  three derivation families distinguished by how psi is held - discrete /
  operational (psi checked: DeepSeek-V4-Pro; the embraOS-QNM Classical
  Approximation, a constructed instance whose honest failure is recorded),
  continuous-manifold (psi conserved or projected: SOL, QNM, and embraOS-QNM-Core,
  a running constructed instance in which psi is a conserved charge of the core's
  own dynamics), and branching-manifold (psi emergent: Many-Worlds) - together
  with a substrate derivation (recursive nesting / the Void) and a diagnostic axis
  (Fringe-Claim Trajectories: the framework pointed at claims that assert an
  unearned identity). The central open problem - a history- or
  trajectory-dependent psi that survives the replica test - is stated as the
  framework's main formal lever; two built instances engage it and neither
  closes it.
